	<body>
		<segment id="1" parent="236" relname="span">SJC 13721 .</segment>
		<segment id="2" parent="1" relname="elaboration-additional">Town of Concord versus Neil Rasmussen and others .</segment>
		<segment id="3" parent="4" relname="topic-solutionhood">Okay , Attorney King , whenever you 're ready .</segment>
		<segment id="4" parent="139" relname="span">All set .</segment>
		<segment id="5" parent="140" relname="joint-other">Good morning .</segment>
		<segment id="6" parent="140" relname="joint-other">May it please the Court .</segment>
		<segment id="7" parent="141" relname="span">Gwen King , on behalf of the appellants .</segment>
		<segment id="8" parent="225" relname="span">Before the Court today is a question</segment>
		<segment id="9" parent="223" relname="joint-list">of both how a way becomes public</segment>
		<segment id="10" parent="224" relname="span">and also how a public way is discontinued</segment>
		<segment id="11" parent="10" relname="causal-result">and ceases to be public .</segment>
		<segment id="12" parent="135" relname="organization-preparation">During my time , I 'd like to address three issues for the Court .</segment>
		<segment id="13" parent="132" relname="span">First , the effect of the 1932 discontinuance by the Middlesex County Commissioners under the 1924 act</segment>
		<segment id="14" parent="13" relname="purpose-attribute">to discontinue public ways .</segment>
		<segment id="15" parent="134" relname="span">Second , whether a way can be deemed public by virtue of a lost layout</segment>
		<segment id="16" parent="133" relname="span">where there is no evidence</segment>
		<segment id="17" parent="16" relname="elaboration-attribute">regarding when it was laid out , who it was laid out by , and most importantly , under what authority it was laid out .</segment>
		<segment id="18" parent="136" relname="span">And third , whether the Court should eliminate the high bar</segment>
		<segment id="19" parent="18" relname="elaboration-attribute">of establishing a public way by prescription ,</segment>
		<segment id="20" parent="136" relname="context-circumstance">where the claim of public prescription is an ancient claim .</segment>
		<segment id="21" parent="145" relname="span">First , as to the effect of the 1932 discontinuance , a fundamental rule of land law is that a way is public</segment>
		<segment id="22" parent="23" relname="organization-phatic">until it is --</segment>
		<segment id="23" parent="144" relname="span">until it is legally discontinued .</segment>
		<segment id="24" parent="222" relname="topic-question">Well , what is a legally -- a legal discontinuance ?</segment>
		<segment id="25" parent="142" relname="span">It is a discontinuance</segment>
		<segment id="26" parent="25" relname="mode-manner">according to law .</segment>
		<segment id="27" parent="142" relname="elaboration-additional">In 1924 , the legislature —</segment>
		<segment id="28" parent="150" relname="organization-preparation">Just to be more specific .</segment>
		<segment id="29" parent="147" relname="span">There are three different ways ,</segment>
		<segment id="30" parent="29" relname="organization-phatic">right ?</segment>
		<segment id="31" parent="148" relname="joint-list">There 's 32A .</segment>
		<segment id="32" parent="148" relname="joint-list">There 's 21 and 30 .</segment>
		<segment id="33" parent="148" relname="organization-phatic">Right ?</segment>
		<segment id="34" parent="152" relname="span">Correct .</segment>
		<segment id="35" parent="36" relname="attribution-negative">Are n't you sort of saying</segment>
		<segment id="36" parent="159" relname="span">all three work exactly the same ?</segment>
		<segment id="37" parent="153" relname="joint-list">I 'm --</segment>
		<segment id="38" parent="153" relname="joint-list">that 's --</segment>
		<segment id="39" parent="158" relname="span">I 'm a little confused by your argument</segment>
		<segment id="40" parent="39" relname="elaboration-attribute">that they 're not doing different things .</segment>
		<segment id="41" parent="157" relname="span">Well , they definitely have different procedures and in some instances different outcomes .</segment>
		<segment id="42" parent="41" relname="explanation-justify">So section 21 , let 's take that first .</segment>
		<segment id="43" parent="155" relname="joint-sequence">Just just pin you down</segment>
		<segment id="44" parent="155" relname="joint-sequence">and I 'll let you talk .</segment>
		<segment id="45" parent="154" relname="span">But my understanding is that your view all three of those turn it into a purely private way .</segment>
		<segment id="46" parent="45" relname="organization-phatic">Right ?</segment>
		<segment id="47" parent="220" relname="span">Well I -- with a slight distinction , Your Honor .</segment>
		<segment id="48" parent="163" relname="span">So under Nylander , this Court held a section 21 discontinuance ,</segment>
		<segment id="49" parent="48" relname="elaboration-attribute">which is a discontinuance at town meeting of a town way .</segment>
		<segment id="50" parent="163" relname="elaboration-additional">And only a town way under section 21 does n't leave any abutter access rights .</segment>
		<segment id="51" parent="217" relname="span">There is no private way</segment>
		<segment id="52" parent="51" relname="elaboration-attribute">left intact after section 21 discontinuance per this court in Nylander .</segment>
		<segment id="53" parent="217" relname="organization-phatic">However —</segment>
		<segment id="54" parent="240" relname="attribution-positive">I thought</segment>
		<segment id="55" parent="56" relname="organization-phatic">the footnote in Nylander is the big , foundation --</segment>
		<segment id="56" parent="240" relname="span">is the foundation of their argument for why this remains still public .</segment>
		<segment id="57" parent="164" relname="span">So the footnote</segment>
		<segment id="58" parent="57" relname="elaboration-attribute">that 's emphasized by the town</segment>
		<segment id="59" parent="165" relname="same-unit">is referring to a subsequent version of 32A .</segment>
		<segment id="60" parent="61" relname="attribution-positive">And in that this court noted</segment>
		<segment id="61" parent="249" relname="span">that that leaves intact public access rights .</segment>
		<segment id="62" parent="63" relname="attribution-positive">Now , we have contended in our brief</segment>
		<segment id="63" parent="166" relname="span">that what the court was construing is public access rights is distinct from how the town characterized it .</segment>
		<segment id="64" parent="214" relname="same-unit">Most notably ,</segment>
		<segment id="65" parent="66" relname="explanation-evidence">as you can see in the underlying appeals court and superior court decision in Nylander public access ,</segment>
		<segment id="66" parent="213" relname="span">private way was referring to abutters ' private rights of access .</segment>
		<segment id="67" parent="242" relname="span">And I emphasized that as an important point</segment>
		<segment id="68" parent="212" relname="same-unit">because ,</segment>
		<segment id="69" parent="168" relname="attribution-positive">as we note ,</segment>
		<segment id="70" parent="167" relname="span">the public right</segment>
		<segment id="71" parent="70" relname="purpose-attribute">to travel</segment>
		<segment id="72" parent="169" relname="span">is the public easement</segment>
		<segment id="73" parent="72" relname="elaboration-attribute">that is laid out under chapter 82 .</segment>
		<segment id="74" parent="210" relname="span">The public right of access is is not something in this court .</segment>
		<segment id="75" parent="243" relname="span">In the 2002 case , Chandler actually noted the distinction</segment>
		<segment id="76" parent="246" relname="attribution-positive">and said ,</segment>
		<segment id="77" parent="244" relname="adversative-antithesis">you ca n't lay out a way for access .</segment>
		<segment id="78" parent="79" relname="organization-phatic">You can onl- --</segment>
		<segment id="79" parent="244" relname="span">the public right is only that of travel ,</segment>
		<segment id="80" parent="208" relname="span">and that 's inconsistent with what is clearly the legislature 's intent in the 1924 act ,</segment>
		<segment id="81" parent="206" relname="attribution-positive">because in the 1924 act , they say</segment>
		<segment id="82" parent="206" relname="joint-list">it 's no longer needed for common convenience and necessity ,</segment>
		<segment id="83" parent="206" relname="joint-list">and it 's not needed to be safe and convenient for travel .</segment>
		<segment id="84" parent="170" relname="span">That 's inconsistent with the concept of what a layout for a public way is needed to be for ,</segment>
		<segment id="85" parent="84" relname="purpose-goal">to be safe and convenient for travel .</segment>
		<segment id="86" parent="209" relname="span">The inverse decision was adjudicated by the county commissioners here ,</segment>
		<segment id="87" parent="86" relname="evaluation-comment">and the adjudication is a very important point as to the distinct meaning of section 32A .</segment>
		<segment id="88" parent="203" relname="organization-phatic">Can you ?</segment>
		<segment id="89" parent="171" relname="span">I have ,</segment>
		<segment id="90" parent="89" relname="organization-phatic">I think ,</segment>
		<segment id="91" parent="172" relname="same-unit">a somewhat related question to Justice Kafka 's question .</segment>
		<segment id="92" parent="198" relname="span">If we look at section 21 ,</segment>
		<segment id="93" parent="92" relname="mode-manner">as it was in effect in 1932 ,</segment>
		<segment id="94" parent="199" relname="span">and section 32A</segment>
		<segment id="95" parent="94" relname="mode-manner">as it was in effect at that time ,</segment>
		<segment id="96" parent="201" relname="span">uh , what is the difference in outcome ?</segment>
		<segment id="97" parent="98" relname="attribution-positive">Are you saying</segment>
		<segment id="98" parent="173" relname="span">that it -- that action under either section would result in exactly the same outcome ?</segment>
		<segment id="99" parent="197" relname="span">No , I 'm not .</segment>
		<segment id="100" parent="197" relname="evaluation-comment">And the question actually shows why section 32A was so important to municipalities and the Commonwealth at the time .</segment>
		<segment id="101" parent="193" relname="adversative-contrast">So first , as to the jurisdiction of section 21 , that only applies to town ways .</segment>
		<segment id="102" parent="174" relname="span">However , the jurisdiction of 32A applies to public ways</segment>
		<segment id="103" parent="102" relname="elaboration-attribute">which the county commissioners had jurisdiction over all public ways at the time .</segment>
		<segment id="104" parent="175" relname="span">And it 's important to go back in time to 1924</segment>
		<segment id="105" parent="104" relname="context-circumstance">when thinking about the jurisdiction of the county commissioners .</segment>
		<segment id="106" parent="176" relname="span">They had authority over public ways</segment>
		<segment id="107" parent="106" relname="elaboration-attribute">being county highways , town ways , and any public way of unknown jurisdiction .</segment>
		<segment id="108" parent="190" relname="span">So that 's an important distinction</segment>
		<segment id="109" parent="189" relname="span">because here</segment>
		<segment id="110" parent="251" relname="span">where ,</segment>
		<segment id="111" parent="110" relname="explanation-evidence">as made clear by the the case before you ,</segment>
		<segment id="112" parent="252" relname="same-unit">what this way was was at minimum uncertain .</segment>
		<segment id="113" parent="177" relname="span">So the county commissioners would have the authority</segment>
		<segment id="114" parent="113" relname="purpose-attribute">to discontinue it under 32A ,</segment>
		<segment id="115" parent="178" relname="span">whereas the town would not have authority</segment>
		<segment id="116" parent="115" relname="purpose-attribute">to discontinue a county way under section 21 .</segment>
		<segment id="117" parent="185" relname="span">I did n't see anything</segment>
		<segment id="118" parent="119" relname="attribution-positive">to suggest</segment>
		<segment id="119" parent="184" relname="span">this was a county way .</segment>
		<segment id="120" parent="180" relname="attribution-positive">Well , to the contrary , the town contends</segment>
		<segment id="121" parent="180" relname="span">that there was a 1763 county layout of the Northern Trail</segment>
		<segment id="122" parent="121" relname="elaboration-attribute">that was effectuated by the county commissioners .</segment>
		<segment id="123" parent="124" relname="attribution-negative">We have disputed the --</segment>
		<segment id="124" parent="182" relname="span">whether that was effective</segment>
		<segment id="125" parent="182" relname="causal-cause">because the conditions -- precedents -- precedents were not fulfilled .</segment>
		<segment id="126" parent="253" relname="span">The town notes in their brief</segment>
		<segment id="127" parent="126" relname="organization-phatic">that --</segment>
		<segment id="128" parent="181" relname="joint-list">and relies</segment>
		<segment id="129" parent="181" relname="joint-list">and emphasizes</segment>
		<segment id="130" parent="131" relname="organization-phatic">that certain deeds of the Northern Trail said --</segment>
		<segment id="131" parent="254" relname="span">referred to the way as a county way .</segment>
